course: dictionary
lesson: 6190
title: Mischievous: a word with a trap
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Mischievous» describes someone who likes playing small tricks in a playful way. A mischievous puppy might chew a slipper and run off, looking pleased with itself. The clue is that the trouble is small and playful, not cruel.
explain: Context can show a second meaning too. A «mischievous grin» tells you that someone is planning a trick, even before anything happens. Ask yourself: is somebody about to do something sneaky?
explain: Be careful with the spelling. Mischievous is spelled M-I-S-C-H-I-E-V-O-U-S and said "MIS-chi-vus". Many people write "mischievious" with an extra i, but there is no i before the -ous ending.
fact: Mischievous means liking to play tricks and cause small trouble in a playful way.
fact: «A mischievous grin» is a smile that shows someone is planning a trick.
fact: Mischievous can also be formal and mean meant to cause harm or trouble, as in «a mischievous rumour».
fact: «Rascal» is a fond word for a mischievous, naughty child.
fact: Spell it M-I-S-C-H-I-E-V-O-U-S: one i after the ch, and no extra i before the -ous.
exercise: Mia sees a «mischievous grin» on her brother's face. What does the grin tell her? | He is going to sleep | He is very sad and lonely | He has lost his shoes | *A trick is being planned
exercise: Which spelling of the word is correct? | mischevious | mischivous | *mischievous | mischievious
exercise: What does «a mischievous rumour» most likely mean? | A happy song for the class | A kind letter from a friend | A rumour about the weather | *A story meant to cause harm or trouble
test: Which word is a fond name for a mischievous, naughty child? | Teacher | Hero | *Rascal | Scholar
test: Which way is the word said out loud? | Mis-CHEE-vus | *MIS-chi-vus | Mis-chiv-ee-us | MIS-chee-vee-us
exam: Which is the correct spelling of the word for playful tricks? | mischievious | *mischievous | mischivous | mischevious
